#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""./submit [--note TEXT]: hand the current commit to the judge.

The judge takes the commit at HEAD of the git repository you are in (changes
not committed are not part of it), copies it to its own directory, waits for
the GPU and keeps it alone for the whole run:
  1. it waits until no process outside the run has the GPU open; on the GPU
     machine it then checks that cand's firewall rule is in place (only
     loopback, no name lookups; controller/box/ij-fw) and stops with an
     error, before any judged prompt is sent, when it is not;
  2. if the tree has a Dockerfile, the judge builds its image (no network;
     $IJ_ENGINE_IMAGE names it); it runs `build` if there is one, starts
     `serve <port>` (on the GPU machine as the account `cand`, which cannot
     read the judge's files; containers only through the Docker launcher);
  3. warm-up: prompts of the same lengths as the judged ones, cut from
     warm-up rows that are never judged, each sent twice;
  4. the engine answers each judged prompt once, one request at a time, all
     in the same request shape (lib/engine.py), each timed on the judge's
     clock.  The judged prompts are drawn fresh for this run from the
     judge's pool and never sent to an engine again;
  5. the engine is stopped and every process of its account killed; then
     the reference walks along each answer: the same measure as ./check;
  6. if that passes: speed = the sum of the answer tokens over the sum of
     the whole-request seconds of those same requests (prompt time
     included); every token counted was checked in step 5;
  7. it records the result; ./score lists it.

Exit code 0 when the submission passed, 1 when it failed, 2 when the judge
could not run it (nothing about the engine is recorded then except why).

  ./submit --reference   (operator) measure the reference engine's speed the
                         same way, on prompts the judge draws, for ./score
  ./submit --tokenize IN OUT   (operator) the reference's token ids for the
                         rows of IN (prompt or messages), written to OUT

The judge on the controller (two-machine design) runs it on the GPU machine
with --judge-src DIR --hidden FILE --warm FILE --out FILE --commit SHA: the
source, the drawn prompts (with prompt_token_ids) and the warm-up rows are
given, the result goes to FILE, no ledger is written.  With --ref-hidden FILE
(a second draw) the reference, once up for the walk, is also timed on those
prompts the same way (result key reference_speed): the machine's reference
speed without a second start of the reference.

With --tasks FILE (rows with token ids, answer and kind), measure B of the
quality bar: after everything timed, the engine answers each task once (not
timed, not in the speed); after the walk the reference answers the same
tasks; both are graded by rule (lib/quality.py).  Measure A comes from the
walk.  While config quality.bar is not set, the result says "quality bar not
set" and PASS or FAIL is the agreement limit's, as before.

Every prompt needs token ids, given or cached, so that the reference starts
once per run; ./submit --tokenize makes them.  After the judged answers,
submit.speed_repeats passes of extra requests give the speed in parts (as in
./check); they never change the score.
"""
